<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<paragraphs xml:id="par-first-principles" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>First-Principles Reasoning</title>
  <idx>first-principles</idx>

   <p>
     Scientific understanding tends to be most durable when constructed from a parsimonious, well-defined set of
     assumptions. At its core, the :logo: <em>Eigenscribe methodology</em> provides a framework for reasoning that
     minimizes treating equations, definitions, and theorems as isolated "black boxes" facts to be memorized. Instead,
     it aims to derive concepts from shared mathematical and physical foundations, making the relationships between
     interdisciplinary ideas explicit. Building knowledge in this manner not only strengthens long-term understanding,
     but also reveals the interconnectedness of scientific disciplines and potentiates the discovery of new connections
     that might otherwise remain obscure. Most importantly, this approach is meant to increase versatility and improve
     adaptability when confronting unfamiliar problems.
   </p>

   <bestpractice xml:id="bp-eigenscribe-reasoning-guidelines">
     <title>Eigenscribe Guidelines</title>
       <p>
         The Eigenscribe methodology is a rigorous framework for reasoning.
         To ensure clarity, rigor, and intellectual honesty, the following principles govern every entry:

         <ol>
           <title>Core Guidelines</title>

           <li><alert>Explicit Assumptions:</alert> Every line of reasoning begins from assumptions that are
           explicitly identified and, whenever possible, justified by empirical observation, canonical physical laws and
           mathematical concepts, or formal axioms.</li>

           <li><alert>Traceable Inference</alert> Every inferential step must be transparent, allowing for independent
           sanity checks and reproduction by the reader.</li>

           <li><alert>Falsifiability</alert> All derived results and conclusions remain provisional and open to
           revision in light of new evidence. The system is designed for iterative correction, where conclusions can
           be refined once new evidence emerges.</li>

           <li><alert>Shared Primitives</alert> Connections between disciplines ("<em>concept bridges</em>")
           are constructed from mathematical structures, physical principles, or computational abstractions rather than
           superficial analogy.</li>

           <li><alert>Ember Warning:</alert> Heuristic arguments, speculative ideas, incomplete derivations, or
           informal analogies that do not satisfy the preceding guidelines should be explicitly marked with the
           :ember: symbol.</li>

           <li><alert>Transparent AI Usage</alert> Artificial intelligence is treated as a collaborative reasoning tool
           rather than an authority. AI-assisted content should be clearly identified and remain subject to the same
           standards of verification and reproducibility as human-generated work.</li>

         </ol>
       </p>
   </bestpractice>



</paragraphs>
